            <ArticleTitle>Assessing pain perception using the Painmatcher in patients with whiplash-associated disorders.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>171-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2340/16501977-0163</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the Painmatcher, in terms of reliability, and to explore the relationship between pain magnitude matching and pain threshold assessments in patients with whiplash-associated disorders. Also, to investigate gender differences in pain thresholds and explore the correlation between pain-related cognitions and pain threshold.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A test-retest study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty-seven patients with whiplash-associated disorders.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">A visual analogue scale and a Painmatcher (an instrument for comparing pain magnitude) were used to evaluate pain intensity. Pain threshold was assessed using the Painmatcher. Pain-related cognitions were assessed using the Painometer and the Tampa Scale for Kinesiophobia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The Painmatcher demonstrated reliable pain magnitude matching scores, but the pain threshold assessment indicated a systematic disagreement. Women exhibited significantly lower pain thresholds than men (p &lt; 0.01). There was a weak but significant correlation between the pain intensity according to the visual analogue scale and the Painmatcher (r = 0.46) (p &lt; 0.01). There was a significant correlation between the emotional experience of pain and pain threshold (r = -0.33) (p &lt; 0.001), but no significant correlation between fear of movement/(re)injury and pain threshold.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Measuring pain with the Painmatcher is a reliable method, but may include a possible bias in threshold assessments and seems to be associated with unpleasantness.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
